// Imaging technologist questions, second batch (Oct 29 2026). Stars mix 2 and 3.
// level|drugId|question|CORRECT answer|wrong|wrong|wrong|explanation
DB.addQ(`
im|iohexol|A patient scheduled for contrast-enhanced CT reports a prior reaction of hives after iodinated contrast. What is the usual approach?|Premedication with a corticosteroid and antihistamine on a timed schedule, per department protocol|Proceed with no changes since hives are minor|Switch to a gadolinium agent for the CT|Cancel all future contrast imaging permanently|A prior mild reaction raises the risk of another, and timed premedication is the standard mitigation.
im|iohexol|Which patient history most increases the risk of contrast-associated kidney injury?|Chronic kidney disease with a reduced eGFR|A shellfish allergy|A prior normal CT with contrast|Controlled hypertension|Baseline renal impairment is the dominant risk factor. Shellfish allergy is not a contrast risk factor.
im|iohexol|A patient's intravenous contrast extravasates into the forearm during injection. What is the first action?|Stop the injection, remove the line and assess the limb|Continue the injection at a slower rate|Massage the area vigorously|Apply a tight compressive bandage|Stopping and assessing comes first; most extravasations are managed conservatively, but compartment syndrome must be excluded.
im|iohexol|Which sign after extravasation suggests a serious complication rather than a routine one?|Increasing pain with a tense compartment, skin blistering or altered sensation|Mild local swelling that is comfortable|A small bruise at the puncture site|Transient coolness of the skin|Compartment syndrome and skin ulceration are the complications that need surgical assessment.
im|metformin|For which patient is metformin typically withheld around iodinated contrast?|One with significantly reduced kidney function or acute kidney injury|Every patient taking metformin|Only patients having MRI|Only patients under 40|Current guidance individualizes this by renal function rather than holding it for everyone.
im|gadobutrol|Which patient group is at risk for nephrogenic systemic fibrosis with gadolinium agents?|Those with severe kidney impairment, especially on dialysis|Those with a penicillin allergy|Those with controlled asthma|Those who are over 65 with normal kidneys|Severe renal impairment is the risk factor, and newer agent groups carry much lower risk.
im|gadobutrol|A patient asks whether gadolinium stays in the body. What is accurate?|Small amounts can be retained in tissue including brain, with no proven clinical harm to date|It is entirely eliminated within one hour with no retention|It causes dementia in most patients|Retention occurs only with iodinated contrast|Retention is documented; clinical significance remains unestablished, and that is the honest answer.
im|epinephrine|During a contrast reaction, a patient has wheezing and hives but a normal blood pressure. What is the usual treatment?|Epinephrine intramuscularly if bronchospasm is significant, with a bronchodilator and oxygen|Diphenhydramine alone in all cases|Steroids alone|Observation only|Significant bronchospasm during a contrast reaction is treated with epinephrine; antihistamines treat the skin only.
im|diphenhydramine|What is diphenhydramine's role in a contrast reaction?|It treats urticaria and itching; it does not treat airway or circulatory compromise|It is the first-line treatment for anaphylaxis|It reverses bronchospasm|It raises blood pressure|Antihistamines address the cutaneous component only, which is why they never substitute for epinephrine.
im|albuterol|A patient develops isolated mild wheezing after contrast, with normal vital signs. A reasonable first step is:|An inhaled beta agonist with oxygen and close monitoring|Immediate intramuscular epinephrine in every case|Intravenous steroids alone|Discharge from the department|Mild isolated bronchospasm can be treated with a bronchodilator, with escalation if it does not settle.
im|atropine|A patient becomes bradycardic and hypotensive after contrast, with warm dry skin and no urticaria. What does this suggest?|A vasovagal reaction rather than anaphylaxis|Anaphylactic shock|Contrast-induced nephropathy|A pulmonary embolism|Bradycardia with hypotension points to a vagal reaction. Anaphylaxis is typically tachycardic with skin findings.
im|atropine|How does the management of a vasovagal reaction differ from anaphylaxis?|Elevate the legs, give fluids, and use atropine for persistent bradycardia rather than epinephrine|Give epinephrine first in both|Withhold fluids in a vagal reaction|Treat both with antihistamines only|Mistaking one for the other leads to the wrong first drug, which is why the distinction matters.
im|tc99m-sestamibi|What instruction matters most before a myocardial perfusion study with a pharmacologic stress agent?|Avoid caffeine and methylxanthines for the period specified by protocol|Fast for 24 hours|Stop all cardiac medications|Drink several liters of water immediately before|Caffeine blocks adenosine receptors and can invalidate the stress portion of the study.
im|regadenoson|A patient becomes severely bronchospastic after regadenoson. What is the specific reversal agent?|Aminophylline|Naloxone|Flumazenil|Protamine|Aminophylline is a competitive antagonist at the adenosine receptor and reverses regadenoson effects.
im|regadenoson|Which patient history is a concern before a regadenoson stress study?|Significant reactive airway disease or high-grade atrioventricular block|Controlled hypothyroidism|A prior normal stress test|Mild seasonal allergies|Adenosine receptor agonists can cause bronchospasm and conduction block.
im|tc99m-sestamibi|A patient is receiving therapeutic radioiodine. Which instruction is part of the standard discharge guidance?|Time and distance precautions from others, especially children and pregnant people|Complete isolation in a lead room for a month|No precautions are needed|Avoid all fluids for 48 hours|Time, distance and simple hygiene measures are the practical radiation protection instructions.
im|tc99m-sestamibi|Why is blood glucose checked before an FDG PET study?|High glucose competes with the tracer and degrades image quality|It determines the radiation dose|It identifies contrast allergy|It predicts extravasation|FDG is a glucose analog, so hyperglycemia reduces tumor uptake and study quality.
im|gadobutrol|Before an MRI with contrast, which screening question is essential?|Renal function and any implanted or embedded metal|Recent dental cleaning|Caffeine intake|Last meal eaten|Renal function governs gadolinium safety, and metal screening governs MRI safety itself.
im|iohexol|A patient on hemodialysis with no residual renal function needs contrast CT. What is generally true?|Contrast can usually be given, because there is no remaining renal function to protect|Contrast is absolutely contraindicated|Emergency dialysis must immediately follow in all cases|Only gadolinium may be used|With no residual function there is nothing left to injure; routine urgent dialysis afterward is not required.
im|methylprednisolone|What is the limitation of corticosteroid premedication before contrast?|It needs hours to work, so it does not help in an emergency scan|It works within five minutes|It prevents all reactions completely|It is given only intravenously|The timed schedule exists because steroids take hours; urgent studies use alternative strategies.
im|iohexol|A patient asks whether a shellfish allergy means they are allergic to contrast. What is accurate?|There is no specific link; the iodine in shellfish is not what causes allergy|Shellfish allergy means certain contrast allergy|Only gadolinium is safe in shellfish allergy|All iodine-containing foods must be avoided|This is a persistent myth. Any atopic history raises general reaction risk slightly, but shellfish is not special.
im|barium-sulfate|When should a water-soluble contrast agent be used instead of barium?|When perforation of the bowel is suspected|For every fluoroscopic study|Only in pediatric patients|When the patient has a contrast allergy|Barium in the peritoneum causes a severe inflammatory reaction, so suspected perforation calls for a water-soluble agent.
im|glucagon|Why is glucagon sometimes given during a gastrointestinal imaging study?|It relaxes smooth muscle and reduces bowel motion artifact|It raises blood pressure during the study|It enhances the contrast signal directly|It prevents allergic reactions|Glucagon's smooth muscle relaxation is used to reduce peristalsis.
im|glucagon|Which patient history warrants caution before giving glucagon for imaging?|Pheochromocytoma or insulinoma|A history of migraine|Controlled hypothyroidism|Prior appendectomy|Glucagon can precipitate a hypertensive crisis in pheochromocytoma and hypoglycemia in insulinoma.
im|epinephrine|In a contrast reaction with hypotension and airway swelling, what dose and route of epinephrine is standard for an adult?|0.3 mg of the 1 mg/mL concentration intramuscularly in the lateral thigh|1 mg of the 1 mg/mL concentration intravenously|0.3 mg of the 0.1 mg/mL concentration intravenously|3 mg intramuscularly|Confusing the concentrations is a well-known fatal error. The intramuscular dose uses the 1 mg/mL product.
im||What is the role of oxygen in managing a moderate contrast reaction?|It is given early alongside the specific drug treatment|It replaces the need for epinephrine|It is withheld until the reaction resolves|It is used only in cardiac arrest|Oxygen is a standard early measure in any significant reaction.
im|iohexol|Which factor increases the risk of a contrast extravasation?|A small distal vein, a power injector at high flow rate, and a patient who cannot report pain|A large antecubital vein|A hand-injected low-volume study|A fully alert, communicative patient|Site, flow rate and the patient's ability to report symptoms are the practical risk factors.
im|tc99m-sestamibi|A technologist is pregnant and asks about working with radiopharmaceuticals. What is the correct approach?|Declare the pregnancy so dose limits and monitoring are applied, following facility policy|Stop all work immediately without discussion|Continue with no changes|Rely on the lead apron alone|Declared pregnancy triggers specific dose limits and monitoring under radiation safety regulations.
im|iohexol|A patient reports a metallic taste and a warm flushing sensation during contrast injection. What is this?|A common physiologic effect, not an allergic reaction|An early sign of anaphylaxis|A sign of extravasation|Evidence of kidney injury|These sensations are expected and are not a reaction. Recognizing that avoids unnecessary stopping of a study.
im|gadobutrol|Why does MRI require screening even for staff entering the magnet room?|The static field is always on and attracts ferromagnetic objects with lethal force|The field is only on during scanning|Screening applies to patients only|The field affects only electronic devices|The magnet is never off, and projectile injuries are a known cause of death in MRI suites.
`);

