DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {"montelukast": {"origin": "Montelukast grew out of decades of work on a mystery substance. In 1938 researchers described a slow-reacting substance released in the lungs during allergic reactions that caused long-lasting airway squeezing. In 1979 Bengt Samuelsson and colleagues in Sweden showed that this slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis was a group of molecules made from arachidonic acid, which they named leukotrienes. Samuelsson shared the 1982 Nobel Prize. Drug companies then raced to block leukotrienes. Merck scientists in Canada designed montelukast to block the cysteinyl leukotriene receptor and to be taken once a day. The FDA approved it as Singulair in 1998. It became one of the most prescribed asthma pills. In 2020 the FDA added a boxed warning about serious mental health side effects.", "timeline": [["1938", "A slow-reacting substance released in allergic reactions is described."], ["1979", "Samuelsson's group identifies the slow-reacting substance as leukotrienes."], ["1998", "FDA approves montelukast (Singulair)."], ["2020", "FDA adds a boxed warning for serious neuropsychiatric events."]], "cell": "When mast cells and eosinophils are activated, the enzyme 5-lipoxygenase converts arachidonic acid to leukotriene A4, which is turned into LTC4, LTD4 and LTE4, the cysteinyl leukotrienes. These bind the CysLT1 receptor, a G protein coupled receptor on airway smooth muscle and inflammatory cells. Activation raises intracellular calcium, causing strong, slow bronchoconstriction, and promotes mucus secretion, vascular leak and eosinophil recruitment. Montelukast is a selective, competitive antagonist of the CysLT1 receptor. By blocking it, montelukast reduces bronchoconstriction, airway swelling, mucus and eosinophilic inflammation. It does not block leukotriene B4, which acts on a different receptor, and it does not affect histamine.", "body": [["Airways", "Reduces leukotriene-driven bronchoconstriction, mucus production and swelling, which lowers asthma symptoms and prevents exercise-induced bronchospasm."], ["Immune cells", "Reduces eosinophil recruitment and activity in the airway lining."], ["Nose", "Reduces congestion and swelling in allergic rhinitis."], ["Brain", "Rarely causes mood changes, agitation, vivid dreams, sleep problems and suicidal thoughts, the basis of the boxed warning."]], "journey": "Montelukast is taken by mouth as a tablet, chewable tablet or granules and is well absorbed. It is more than 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down mainly with CYP2C8, with help from CYP3A4 and CYP2C9, and the metabolites leave mostly in the bile and feces, with very little in the urine. The half-life is about three to six hours, but the clinical effect lasts long enough for once-daily dosing, usually in the evening. It is a controller, not a rescue drug, because its effect builds rather than acting within minutes.", "sidefx": "Montelukast is usually well tolerated; headache and abdominal pain are the most common complaints. The most important concern is neuropsychiatric: agitation, aggression, depression, nightmares, sleepwalking and suicidal thinking have been reported in children and adults. The exact cause is not established, but leukotriene receptors are present in the brain. Because of this boxed warning, it is not preferred for mild allergic rhinitis alone. Rarely, eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis has appeared, often when oral steroids are tapered, which may unmask the disease rather than cause it. There is no antidote.", "fun": "The name leukotriene comes from leukocytes, the white blood cells that make them, and triene, the three conjugated double bonds in their structure.", "src": ["Singulair (montelukast) FDA label", "Samuelsson B. Leukotrienes: mediators of immediate hypersensitivity reactions and inflammation. Science 1983", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "naloxone": {"origin": "Naloxone is a synthetic molecule built from the opioid oxymorphone. Around 1960 Jack Fishman and Mozes Lewenstein, working in New York, made it by replacing a methyl group on the nitrogen with an allyl group. That small change turned a strong opioid into a pure blocker with no opioid effect of its own. A patent was filed in 1961, and Endo Laboratories developed it. The FDA approved naloxone injection as Narcan in 1971. Earlier opioid antagonists such as nalorphine had partial opioid effects and could worsen breathing, so a pure antagonist was a major advance. During the opioid overdose epidemic, community programs began giving naloxone to laypeople. The FDA approved Narcan nasal spray in 2015, and in 2023 approved it for sale over the counter without a prescription.", "timeline": [["1961", "A patent is filed for naloxone, made by Jack Fishman and Mozes Lewenstein."], ["1971", "FDA approves naloxone injection (Narcan)."], ["2015", "FDA approves Narcan nasal spray."], ["2023", "FDA approves Narcan nasal spray for over-the-counter sale."]], "cell": "Naloxone is a competitive antagonist at opioid receptors, with highest affinity for the mu receptor and lower affinity for kappa and delta. It binds the same pocket as opioids but does not activate the receptor's G protein, so it has no effect of its own. Because it binds tightly and is given at high receptor occupancy, it displaces opioids such as heroin, morphine and fentanyl from mu receptors. Once displaced, the Gi signaling stops: adenylyl cyclase is no longer inhibited, calcium channels reopen, and potassium channels close, so brainstem respiratory neurons fire normally again. Breathing and alertness return. In someone who is physically dependent, the sudden receptor emptying unmasks the adaptations of tolerance, producing an acute withdrawal state.", "body": [["Breathing", "Restores the brainstem's response to carbon dioxide, so breathing rate and depth return, often within minutes."], ["Brain", "Reverses opioid sedation and coma; in dependent patients it causes sudden withdrawal with agitation and craving."], ["Heart and vessels", "Sudden reversal causes a surge of catecholamines, which can raise heart rate and blood pressure and, rarely, cause arrhythmias."], ["Lungs", "Rarely, abrupt reversal is followed by noncardiogenic pulmonary edema, linked to the catecholamine surge."], ["Gut and skin", "Withdrawal brings vomiting, diarrhea, cramps, sweating and goosebumps."]], "journey": "Naloxone works IV within about two minutes, and IM, under the skin or in the nose within several minutes. By mouth it is almost entirely destroyed by first-pass liver metabolism, which is why it is added to some oral opioid products to discourage injection. It is very fat soluble and enters the brain quickly. The liver glucuronidates it, and the metabolite leaves in the urine. Its half-life is short, about one hour, and its effect often ends in 30 to 90 minutes. Many opioids, especially long-acting ones, last longer, so patients can slip back into respiratory depression after naloxone wears off.", "sidefx": "Naloxone itself has very little toxicity in someone who has not taken opioids. Its side effects come from what it removes. In opioid-dependent people it causes precipitated withdrawal: agitation, vomiting, diarrhea, sweating, goosebumps, fast heart rate and pain. Vomiting with a decreased level of consciousness risks aspiration. The rush of catecholamines can cause high blood pressure, arrhythmias and, rarely, pulmonary edema. That is why many protocols ventilate with a bag-valve mask first and titrate to breathing, not to full alertness. Recurrent sedation after it wears off is the other main risk.", "fun": "Changing just one group on the nitrogen of oxymorphone, from methyl to allyl, is what turns a powerful opioid into its own antidote.", "src": ["Naloxone hydrochloride injection FDA label", "Narcan nasal spray FDA labeling", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]}, "naproxen": {"origin": "Naproxen is a synthetic NSAID from the propionic acid family, the same family as ibuprofen. It was developed at Syntex, a company in Palo Alto, California, in the late 1960s. Syntex chemists were looking for an anti-inflammatory drug that lasted longer than aspirin and ibuprofen, so patients could take it fewer times a day. Naproxen is made as a single mirror-image form (the S enantiomer), which is the active one. The FDA approved it by prescription as Naprosyn in 1976, and the sodium salt, Anaprox, followed. In 1994 the FDA approved naproxen sodium for over-the-counter sale as Aleve. Its long half-life made twice-daily dosing possible, a major reason for its popularity in arthritis.", "timeline": [["1976", "FDA approves naproxen (Naprosyn) by prescription."], ["1994", "Naproxen sodium becomes available over the counter as Aleve."]], "cell": "Cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes convert arachidonic acid, released from cell membranes by phospholipase A2, into prostaglandin H2, the parent of prostaglandins and thromboxane. COX-1 is always active in many tissues and makes prostaglandins that protect the stomach lining, support kidney blood flow and help platelets make thromboxane A2. COX-2 is induced at sites of inflammation and makes prostaglandins that cause pain, swelling and fever. Naproxen reversibly and nonselectively inhibits both COX-1 and COX-2 by blocking the channel where arachidonic acid enters the enzyme. Less PGE2 at injured tissue means less sensitization of pain nerve endings and less vasodilation and swelling. Less PGE2 in the hypothalamus lowers the fever set point. Blocking COX-1 explains its stomach, kidney and platelet effects.", "body": [["Joints and tissues", "Reduces prostaglandin-driven swelling, warmth and pain in arthritis, injuries, gout and menstrual cramps."], ["Brain", "Lowers prostaglandin E2 in the hypothalamus, which lowers fever."], ["Stomach", "Loss of protective prostaglandins reduces mucus, bicarbonate and blood flow in the lining, raising the risk of ulcers and bleeding."], ["Kidneys", "Prostaglandins keep kidney arterioles open when blood flow is low; blocking them can cause acute kidney injury, fluid retention and high potassium."], ["Platelets", "Blocking platelet COX-1 lowers thromboxane while the drug is present, mildly increasing bleeding."], ["Heart and vessels", "Can raise blood pressure and carries a boxed warning for heart attack and stroke risk, shared by all NSAIDs."]], "journey": "Naproxen is taken by mouth and is almost completely absorbed; the sodium salt dissolves faster and works sooner. It is more than 99 percent bound to albumin, so it can displace other highly bound drugs. It reaches joint fluid well and crosses the placenta. The liver demethylates it using CYP2C9 and CYP1A2, and both the drug and its metabolites are glucuronidated and excreted in the urine. The half-life is long, about 12 to 17 hours, which is why it is taken only once or twice a day.", "sidefx": "Stomach ulcers and GI bleeding happen because COX-1 prostaglandins protect the stomach lining, and naproxen blocks them. Kidney injury, sodium and water retention and high potassium follow from loss of kidney prostaglandins, especially in dehydration, heart failure or with ACE inhibitors and diuretics. Blood pressure can rise. All NSAIDs carry boxed warnings for cardiovascular events and GI bleeding. Some people with asthma and nasal polyps develop bronchospasm because blocking COX shifts arachidonic acid toward leukotrienes. In late pregnancy it can cause premature closure of the fetal ductus arteriosus. There is no specific antidote.", "src": ["Naprosyn (naproxen) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "nitroglycerin": {"origin": "Nitroglycerin was first made in 1847 by the Italian chemist Ascanio Sobrero, who mixed glycerol with nitric and sulfuric acids. He found it dangerously explosive, and he also noted that a tiny taste gave him a pounding headache. Alfred Nobel later tamed it into dynamite in 1867. Physicians had already seen that amyl nitrite, described by Thomas Lauder Brunton in 1867, relieved angina. In 1879 the London physician William Murrell published a report in The Lancet showing that small amounts of dilute nitroglycerin under the tongue relieved angina. How it worked stayed a mystery for a century, until Robert Furchgott, Louis Ignarro and Ferid Murad showed that nitric oxide is a signaling molecule that relaxes blood vessels. They shared the 1998 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.", "timeline": [["1847", "Ascanio Sobrero synthesizes nitroglycerin."], ["1867", "Alfred Nobel patents dynamite; Lauder Brunton uses amyl nitrite for angina."], ["1879", "William Murrell reports nitroglycerin for angina in The Lancet."], ["1998", "Furchgott, Ignarro and Murad share the Nobel Prize for discovering nitric oxide signaling."]], "cell": "Nitroglycerin is an organic nitrate that releases nitric oxide (NO) inside vascular smooth muscle. This bioactivation involves enzymes including mitochondrial aldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH2). NO activates soluble guanylate cyclase, which converts GTP to cyclic GMP. Cyclic GMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers intracellular calcium and activates myosin light chain phosphatase. Myosin light chains are dephosphorylated, and the smooth muscle relaxes. Veins are most sensitive, so at lower levels it mainly dilates veins, pooling blood and reducing preload. At higher levels it also dilates arteries, reducing afterload, and it dilates large coronary arteries and relieves spasm. Less preload means a smaller, less stretched ventricle that uses less oxygen, which relieves angina. Continuous exposure causes tolerance, partly from reduced bioactivation.", "body": [["Veins", "Venous dilation pools blood in the veins, lowering preload and the volume the heart must pump, which reduces oxygen demand and relieves pulmonary congestion."], ["Arteries", "At higher levels, arterial dilation lowers afterload and blood pressure."], ["Coronary arteries", "Dilates large coronary arteries and collateral vessels and relieves spasm, improving blood flow to ischemic areas."], ["Brain", "Dilation of meningeal vessels causes a throbbing headache, the most common side effect."], ["Heart rate", "Falling blood pressure can trigger reflex tachycardia; in some patients a vagal reflex causes sudden bradycardia and fainting."]], "journey": "Nitroglycerin is absorbed very quickly through the mouth lining when placed under the tongue or sprayed, and works within one to three minutes. Swallowed nitroglycerin is mostly destroyed by first-pass liver metabolism, which is why sublingual, IV, transdermal patch and ointment routes are used. It is broken down rapidly in the liver, blood vessel walls and red cells into dinitrates and mononitrates, which leave through the kidneys. The half-life is only a few minutes, so IV effects stop quickly after the infusion is stopped. IV nitroglycerin can be absorbed into PVC tubing, so special tubing is used.", "sidefx": "Headache, flushing and dizziness are direct results of vasodilation. Low blood pressure and fainting come from venous pooling and reduced preload, which is especially dangerous in right ventricular infarction, which depends on preload, and in severe aortic stenosis. Combining nitrates with PDE-5 inhibitors such as sildenafil causes severe, prolonged hypotension because both drugs raise cGMP. Reflex tachycardia can increase oxygen demand. Tolerance develops with continuous use, so patches are removed for part of each day. Very high exposure can cause methemoglobinemia, treated with methylene blue. Hypotension is treated by lying the patient flat, raising the legs and giving fluids.", "fun": "Alfred Nobel, who made his fortune from nitroglycerin explosives, was later prescribed nitroglycerin for his own chest pain.", "src": ["Nitrostat (nitroglycerin sublingual) FDA label", "Murrell W. Nitro-glycerine as a remedy for angina pectoris. Lancet 1879", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]}, "omeprazole": {"origin": "Omeprazole was developed by the Swedish company Astra, at its Hassle research unit near Gothenburg. In the 1970s the team was studying compounds that reduced stomach acid, starting from a molecule called timoprazole. Around the same time, researchers identified the hydrogen-potassium ATPase, the proton pump, as the final step in acid secretion. The Astra team, including Ivan Ostholm, Enar Carlsson and Bjorn Wallmark, refined the benzimidazole chemistry and synthesized omeprazole in 1979. It was launched in Europe as Losec in 1988 and approved by the FDA in 1989. In the US the name was changed to Prilosec in 1990 after mix-ups with the diuretic Lasix. It became the first proton pump inhibitor, one of the best-selling drugs in the world, and went over the counter in 2003.", "timeline": [["1979", "Astra Hassle scientists synthesize omeprazole."], ["1988", "Omeprazole is launched in Europe as Losec."], ["1989", "FDA approves omeprazole."], ["1990", "The US brand name changes to Prilosec to avoid confusion with Lasix."], ["2003", "Omeprazole becomes available over the counter."]], "cell": "Parietal cells in the stomach secrete acid using the H+/K+ ATPase, the proton pump, which swaps hydrogen ions into the stomach for potassium ions. This is the final common step of acid secretion, regardless of whether gastrin, histamine or acetylcholine is the signal. Omeprazole is a prodrug and a weak base. It concentrates in the very acidic space of the parietal cell canaliculi, where acid converts it to a reactive sulfenamide. That active form binds covalently to cysteine residues on the pump, forming disulfide bonds that permanently inactivate it. Acid secretion resumes only when the cell makes new pumps, which takes about a day or more. Because it acts on pumps that are active, it works best when taken before a meal, when pumps are being activated.", "body": [["Stomach", "Strongly suppresses acid production, allowing ulcers and esophagitis to heal and improving H. pylori eradication when combined with antibiotics."], ["Esophagus", "Less acid reflux reduces heartburn and allows the esophageal lining to heal."], ["Gut flora and infection", "Less acid removes a barrier to swallowed bacteria, raising the risk of C. difficile infection and some pneumonias."], ["Nutrient absorption", "Long-term acid suppression reduces absorption of vitamin B12, iron, calcium and magnesium, and is linked to fractures."], ["Hormones", "Low acid triggers increased gastrin release, which can cause rebound acid when stopped."]], "journey": "Omeprazole is taken by mouth in an enteric-coated form so stomach acid does not destroy it before absorption in the small intestine. It is absorbed quickly, but its blood half-life is only about one hour. Its effect lasts much longer, about a day or more, because it permanently disables pumps. Full effect builds over a few days. The liver metabolizes it mainly with CYP2C19 and also CYP3A4, and the inactive metabolites leave mainly in the urine. People who are poor CYP2C19 metabolizers have higher levels. Omeprazole inhibits CYP2C19, which can reduce activation of clopidogrel.", "sidefx": "Headache, diarrhea and abdominal pain are common short-term effects. Most long-term concerns trace to low stomach acid: higher risk of C. difficile infection, poor absorption of vitamin B12 and iron, and low magnesium, which can cause muscle cramps and arrhythmias. Fracture risk is modestly increased with long use. Rarely, it causes acute interstitial nephritis, an immune reaction in the kidney. Because the body raises gastrin in response, acid can rebound when it is stopped. CYP2C19 inhibition can reduce clopidogrel's effect. There is no antidote.", "src": ["Prilosec (omeprazole) FDA label", "Olbe L, Carlsson E, Lindberg P. A proton-pump inhibitor expedition: the case histories of omeprazole and esomeprazole. Nat Rev Drug Discov 2003", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "ondansetron": {"origin": "Ondansetron came from research into serotonin receptors at Glaxo in the UK in the early 1980s. Chemotherapy drugs such as cisplatin caused severe vomiting, and the main antiemetic then, high-dose metoclopramide, caused movement side effects. Scientists learned that cisplatin damages cells in the gut lining, which release serotonin, and that serotonin acting on 5-HT3 receptors triggers vomiting. Glaxo scientists designed ondansetron as a selective 5-HT3 receptor blocker. The FDA approved it as Zofran in 1991 for chemotherapy nausea, and approvals for postoperative and radiation-induced nausea followed. It transformed chemotherapy, allowing many patients to finish treatment that they would otherwise have refused. A dissolving tablet made it easy to use in children and in EMS.", "timeline": [["1980s", "Glaxo scientists develop ondansetron as a selective 5-HT3 antagonist."], ["1991", "FDA approves ondansetron (Zofran) for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting."], ["2011", "FDA issues a safety warning about QT prolongation with ondansetron."]], "cell": "5-HT3 receptors are unusual among serotonin receptors: they are ligand-gated ion channels, not G protein coupled receptors. When serotonin binds, the channel opens and lets sodium and potassium through, depolarizing the neuron. Chemotherapy, radiation and some toxins damage enterochromaffin cells in the gut lining, which release large amounts of serotonin. That serotonin activates 5-HT3 receptors on vagal afferent nerves, which carry signals to the nucleus tractus solitarius and the chemoreceptor trigger zone in the area postrema, which in turn activate the vomiting center. Ondansetron is a selective competitive antagonist of 5-HT3 receptors both on the vagal nerve endings in the gut and in the chemoreceptor trigger zone. It blocks the signal at both ends, preventing nausea and vomiting. It does not block dopamine receptors.", "body": [["Gut nerves", "Blocks serotonin from activating vagal nerve endings in the gut, cutting off a key trigger for vomiting."], ["Brainstem", "Blocks 5-HT3 receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone and nucleus tractus solitarius, reducing the vomiting reflex."], ["Heart", "Blocks cardiac potassium channels slightly, which can prolong the QT interval and, rarely, trigger torsades de pointes."], ["Bowel", "Slows colonic transit, which can cause constipation."], ["Head", "Headache is the most common side effect."]], "journey": "Ondansetron can be given IV, IM, by mouth or as a tablet that dissolves on the tongue. Oral bioavailability is about 60 percent because of first-pass metabolism. IV doses act within minutes, and oral or dissolving tablets act within about half an hour. It is moderately protein bound. The liver breaks it down with several enzymes, mainly CYP3A4, with CYP1A2 and CYP2D6, so no single inhibitor changes it much. Ultrarapid CYP2D6 metabolizers may clear it faster and get less benefit. Metabolites leave in the urine and feces. The half-life is about three to six hours, longer in severe liver disease.", "sidefx": "Headache and constipation are the most common side effects; constipation reflects slowed colonic movement from 5-HT3 blockade. QT prolongation happens because ondansetron also blocks the hERG potassium channel that helps heart cells repolarize; the risk is higher with large IV doses, low potassium or magnesium, congenital long QT and other QT-prolonging drugs. Serotonin syndrome has been reported when it is combined with other serotonergic drugs. Because it has no dopamine blockade, it does not cause the movement side effects of metoclopramide or prochlorperazine. There is no antidote; torsades is treated with magnesium.", "src": ["Zofran (ondansetron) FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on ondansetron and QT prolongation, 2011", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}});

;
