Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {"prednisone": {"origin": "Prednisone was made at Schering Corporation in the early 1950s by Arthur Nobile's team, who used the bacterium Corynebacterium simplex to add a double bond at carbons 1 and 2 of cortisone. This small change made a drug that was about four times stronger than cortisone against inflammation, with less salt retention. Schering launched it in 1955 as Meticorten, and prednisolone was released at the same time. Prednisone became cheap, reliable and one of the most prescribed drugs in the world. It treats asthma, COPD flares, autoimmune disease, allergic reactions, some cancers and organ transplant rejection. It is a prodrug: the liver must turn it into prednisolone before it works.", "timeline": [["1948", "Philip Hench and colleagues at Mayo Clinic first give cortisone to a patient with rheumatoid arthritis."], ["1950s", "Arthur Nobile at Schering makes prednisone from cortisone using bacterial fermentation."], ["1955", "Prednisone is marketed in the US as Meticorten."]], "cell": "Prednisone has a keto group at carbon 11 and cannot bind the glucocorticoid receptor well. In the liver, the enzyme 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 1 reduces it to prednisolone, the active form. Prednisolone enters target cells and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor. The complex moves into the nucleus and changes gene transcription. It turns on anti-inflammatory genes such as annexin A1 (which blocks phospholipase A2 and the arachidonic acid cascade) and IkappaB (which holds NF-kappaB in check), and it represses NF-kappaB and AP-1 driven genes for IL-1, IL-2, IL-6, TNF-alpha and COX-2. T cells are suppressed and many lymphocytes undergo apoptosis. Effects build over hours as protein levels change.", "body": [["Immune system", "It lowers cytokines, shrinks lymphocyte and eosinophil counts, and stops white cells from entering tissues, calming autoimmune disease and allergy and preventing transplant rejection."], ["White blood cells", "Neutrophil counts rise because cells are released from bone marrow and stop sticking to vessel walls, which is expected and not a sign of infection."], ["Lungs", "Less airway inflammation shortens asthma and COPD flares."], ["Metabolism", "Higher liver glucose output and insulin resistance raise blood sugar. Appetite and weight increase, and fat moves to the face, neck and abdomen."], ["Stomach", "It reduces protective prostaglandins, so ulcer and bleeding risk rises, especially with NSAIDs."], ["Bone, skin and eyes", "Long use causes bone loss, thin skin, easy bruising, poor wound healing, cataracts and glaucoma."], ["Adrenal glands", "It suppresses the HPA axis, so long courses must be tapered to let the adrenal glands recover."]], "journey": "Prednisone is taken by mouth and absorbed well. The liver quickly converts it to prednisolone, so in severe liver disease the conversion may be less reliable and prednisolone may be preferred. The active drug binds plasma proteins, spreads widely and crosses the placenta, where placental enzymes inactivate much of it. Prednisolone is metabolized in the liver, partly through CYP3A4, and metabolites leave in urine. The plasma half-life is about two to three hours, but the biologic effect lasts 12 to 36 hours, which allows once-daily morning dosing that mimics the body's own cortisol rhythm.", "sidefx": "Side effects reflect the many cells that carry glucocorticoid receptors. Immune suppression raises the risk of infections and reactivation of TB or hepatitis B. Gluconeogenesis drives high blood sugar. Effects on the brain cause insomnia, mood swings and sometimes psychosis. Mild mineralocorticoid activity causes fluid retention and high blood pressure. Bone loss comes from reduced osteoblast work and lower calcium absorption. After weeks of use, the adrenal glands are suppressed, so stopping suddenly can cause adrenal crisis with low blood pressure and shock, treated with IV hydrocortisone. There is no antidote.", "fun": "Edward Kendall, Philip Hench and Tadeus Reichstein shared the 1950 Nobel Prize for adrenal hormone work, and prednisone was one of the first improved steroids to follow.", "src": ["Prednisone tablets FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}, "sertraline": {"origin": "Sertraline was discovered at Pfizer in Groton, Connecticut, in the late 1970s by chemists Kenneth Koe and Willard Welch. They were exploring a series of compounds first studied as possible antidepressants that blocked norepinephrine reuptake, and found that one form of a molecule was a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor. Development was slow, but after fluoxetine (Prozac) proved the SSRI concept, Pfizer pushed sertraline forward. The FDA approved it as Zoloft in 1991. It later became the first drug approved by the FDA for PTSD in 1999. Sertraline became one of the most prescribed antidepressants in the US because it works for depression, panic disorder, OCD, social anxiety, PTSD and premenstrual dysphoric disorder.", "timeline": [["1970s", "Kenneth Koe and Willard Welch discover sertraline at Pfizer."], ["1991", "FDA approves sertraline (Zoloft) for depression."], ["1999", "Sertraline becomes the first FDA-approved drug for PTSD."], ["2004", "FDA adds a boxed warning about suicidality in young people for antidepressants."]], "cell": "Sertraline selectively blocks the serotonin transporter (SERT) on presynaptic serotonin neurons. SERT normally pumps serotonin back into the neuron after release. With SERT blocked, serotonin stays longer in the synapse and acts on more receptors. At first, extra serotonin also activates 5-HT1A autoreceptors on cell bodies in the raphe nuclei, which reduces neuron firing. Over two to six weeks, these autoreceptors become desensitized, so serotonin neurons fire normally while reuptake stays blocked. This leads to sustained higher serotonin signaling, along with downstream changes such as increased BDNF and nerve cell remodeling, which match the delayed mood benefit. Sertraline also weakly blocks dopamine reuptake, more than most SSRIs, and binds sigma-1 receptors.", "body": [["Brain (mood and anxiety)", "Over weeks, sustained serotonin signaling improves depression, panic attacks, obsessive-compulsive symptoms and PTSD."], ["Gut", "Most of the body's serotonin is in the gut, so extra serotonin causes nausea, loose stools and diarrhea, especially at the start."], ["Sexual function", "Serotonin effects in the spinal cord and brain can delay orgasm and lower libido."], ["Blood clotting", "Platelets take up serotonin through SERT, so blocking it lowers platelet serotonin and raises bleeding risk, especially with NSAIDs or anticoagulants."], ["Sodium balance", "It can cause SIADH and low sodium, particularly in older adults."], ["Sleep", "It can cause insomnia or, in some people, drowsiness."]], "journey": "Sertraline is taken by mouth and absorbed slowly, with peak levels about four and a half to eight hours after a dose. It is about 98 percent protein bound and crosses the blood-brain barrier and placenta. The liver metabolizes it through several CYP enzymes, including CYP2B6, CYP2C19 and CYP3A4, into desmethylsertraline, which is much weaker. Because many enzymes share the work, it has fewer interactions than fluoxetine or paroxetine, though it mildly inhibits CYP2D6. Its half-life is about 26 hours, allowing once-daily dosing. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Mood effects take two to six weeks to appear.", "sidefx": "GI upset comes from serotonin receptors in the gut. Sexual dysfunction and insomnia come from serotonin effects in the brain and spinal cord. Bleeding risk comes from lower platelet serotonin. Hyponatremia comes from SIADH. Serotonin syndrome can occur when it is combined with MAO inhibitors, linezolid, tramadol, triptans or other serotonin drugs, and it is treated by stopping the drugs and giving cyproheptadine and supportive care. Antidepressants carry a boxed warning for suicidal thoughts in children and young adults. Stopping suddenly can cause discontinuation symptoms such as dizziness, irritability and electric shock sensations, so it is tapered.", "src": ["Zoloft (sertraline) FDA label", "Stahl's Essential Psychopharmacology"]}});

;
