Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {"warfarin": {"origin": "In the 1920s cattle in the northern US and Canada began bleeding to death after eating spoiled sweet clover hay. Veterinarians Frank Schofield and Lee Roderick linked the disease to moldy clover. In 1933 a Wisconsin farmer brought a dead heifer and a milk can of unclotted blood to the lab of Karl Paul Link at the University of Wisconsin. Link's team spent years isolating the cause, and in 1940 identified dicoumarol, formed when mold acts on natural coumarin in clover. Link then made stronger analogs. One became a rat poison in 1948, named warfarin after the Wisconsin Alumni Research Foundation (WARF), which funded the work. After a man survived a large suicide attempt with it, warfarin was tested in people. It was approved for human use in 1954 as Coumadin. In 1955 it was given to President Eisenhower after his heart attack.", "timeline": [["1920s", "Sweet clover disease kills cattle in the northern US and Canada."], ["1933", "A farmer brings a dead heifer and unclotted blood to Karl Paul Link's lab."], ["1940", "Link's team identifies dicoumarol as the anticoagulant in spoiled clover."], ["1948", "Warfarin is introduced as a rat poison."], ["1954", "Warfarin is approved for human use."], ["1955", "President Eisenhower receives warfarin after his heart attack."]], "cell": "Clotting factors II, VII, IX and X, and the anticoagulant proteins C and S, need gamma-carboxylation of glutamic acid residues to bind calcium and work. The enzyme gamma-glutamyl carboxylase uses reduced vitamin K (vitamin K hydroquinone) as a cofactor, and turns it into vitamin K epoxide. Vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKORC1) recycles it back to the active form. Warfarin inhibits VKORC1. Active vitamin K runs out, so the liver makes clotting factors that cannot be carboxylated and do not work. Warfarin does not affect factors already in the blood, so its full effect appears only as existing factors are cleared, over several days. Factor VII and protein C go first because they have the shortest half-lives.", "body": [["Blood", "Clotting slows and PT/INR rises, which prevents clots in atrial fibrillation, venous thromboembolism and mechanical heart valves."], ["Skin", "Early in therapy, falling protein C before other factors can cause a brief hypercoagulable state and warfarin skin necrosis, especially in protein C deficiency."], ["Gut and urinary tract", "Bleeding from the gut and urinary tract is common when INR is too high."], ["Brain", "Intracranial hemorrhage is the most feared bleeding complication."], ["Fetus", "Warfarin crosses the placenta and can cause birth defects (warfarin embryopathy) and fetal bleeding."], ["Toes", "Rarely, cholesterol emboli cause painful purple toes (purple toe syndrome)."]], "journey": "Warfarin is taken by mouth and absorbed almost completely. It is about 99 percent bound to albumin, so drugs that displace it can raise its effect. It crosses the placenta. It is a racemic mix; the more potent S-warfarin is metabolized mainly by CYP2C9, and R-warfarin by CYP3A4 and CYP1A2. Inactive metabolites leave in urine. The half-life is about 40 hours, and full anticoagulant effect takes about five to seven days. Variants in CYP2C9 and VKORC1 change dose needs, and countless drugs and foods affect INR.", "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main toxicity and a boxed warning, because the drug lowers working clotting factors throughout the body. Skin necrosis comes from early loss of protein C. Drug interactions via CYP2C9 (such as with TMP-SMX, metronidazole and amiodarone) and changes in vitamin K intake cause large INR swings. Reversal is with vitamin K (restores factor synthesis over hours) and, for serious bleeding, four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate or fresh frozen plasma, which replace factors immediately.", "fun": "Warfarin's name comes from WARF (Wisconsin Alumni Research Foundation) plus '-arin' from coumarin.", "src": ["Coumadin (warfarin) FDA label", "Link KP. Circulation 1959", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}});

;
