Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {"pseudoephedrine": {"origin": "Pseudoephedrine comes from Ephedra, a scrubby shrub known in Chinese medicine as ma huang and used for thousands of years for colds, cough and wheezing. In 1885 the Japanese chemist Nagai Nagayoshi isolated ephedrine from the plant. A few years later, in 1889, the German chemists Albert Ladenburg and C. Oelschlagel described a second, closely related alkaloid from Ephedra, pseudoephedrine, which is a stereoisomer of ephedrine. Pseudoephedrine turned out to shrink swollen nasal tissue with less effect on the heart and blood pressure than ephedrine, and it became one of the most common oral decongestants, sold under names such as Sudafed and added to many allergy combinations. Because it can be chemically reduced to methamphetamine, the US Combat Methamphetamine Epidemic Act moved it behind the pharmacy counter with purchase limits in 2006.", "timeline": [["Ancient", "Ephedra (ma huang) is used in Chinese medicine for respiratory complaints."], ["1885", "Nagai Nagayoshi isolates ephedrine from Ephedra."], ["1889", "Ladenburg and Oelschlagel describe pseudoephedrine, a stereoisomer of ephedrine."], ["2006", "The Combat Methamphetamine Epidemic Act moves pseudoephedrine behind the counter with sales limits."]], "cell": "Pseudoephedrine is a mixed-acting sympathomimetic. Its main action is indirect: it is taken up into sympathetic nerve endings and displaces norepinephrine from storage vesicles, so more norepinephrine spills into the synapse. It also has weak direct agonist activity at alpha and beta adrenergic receptors. In the nose, norepinephrine acting on alpha-1 receptors (Gq, phospholipase C, IP3, calcium release) contracts the smooth muscle of the venous sinusoids and arterioles in the turbinates. Less blood pools in these erectile vessels, the mucosa shrinks and the airway opens. The same release of norepinephrine elsewhere raises vascular tone, heart rate and contractility, and its partial entry into the brain causes mild stimulation.", "body": [["Nose and sinuses", "Narrowed blood vessels in the nasal lining reduce swelling, so air moves more freely and sinus openings can drain."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Extra norepinephrine at alpha-1 and beta-1 receptors can raise blood pressure and heart rate and cause palpitations, which matters in hypertension and heart disease."], ["Brain", "Mild central stimulation causes nervousness, restlessness and trouble sleeping, and young children can become agitated or have hallucinations."], ["Bladder", "Alpha-1 stimulation tightens the bladder neck and prostate smooth muscle, which can cause urinary retention in men with an enlarged prostate."]], "journey": "Pseudoephedrine is swallowed and absorbed well from the gut, with effects starting in about half an hour. Immediate-release forms work for several hours, while extended-release tablets spread release over half a day to a full day. The liver changes only a small amount of it (by N-demethylation). Most of the drug leaves unchanged in the urine. The half-life is several hours and depends on urine pH: acidic urine speeds removal, while alkaline urine traps more of the drug in the body and lengthens its effect. Kidney disease also slows its clearance.", "sidefx": "The side effects are the sympathetic nervous system turned up. Norepinephrine release raises blood pressure and heart rate, which explains palpitations and, rarely, arrhythmias, stroke or heart attack in vulnerable people. Constriction of gut vessels can rarely cause ischemic colitis. Central stimulation causes insomnia, anxiety and, in children, hallucinations. Bladder neck contraction explains urinary retention. Combining it with MAO inhibitors is dangerous because they prevent breakdown of the extra norepinephrine, which can cause a hypertensive crisis. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "The pharmacy logbook and ID check for buying cold pills in the US exists because pseudoephedrine was a key ingredient in home methamphetamine labs, and the 2006 law that created it is part of the USA PATRIOT reauthorization act.", "src": ["Sudafed (pseudoephedrine) OTC Drug Facts label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Combat Methamphetamine Epidemic Act of 2005"]}, "dextromethorphan": {"origin": "Dextromethorphan was built in the lab as a safer stand-in for codeine. After World War II, researchers looked for a cough medicine that would quiet the cough reflex without the addiction and breathing risks of opioids. Chemists at Hoffmann-La Roche worked with the morphinan family of molecules. The levo form, levomethorphan, is a strong opioid, but its mirror image, dextromethorphan, barely binds opioid receptors and still suppresses cough. The FDA approved it in 1958, and it became the most widely used over-the-counter cough suppressant in the US. Later its other actions led to new uses: combined with quinidine as Nuedexta for pseudobulbar affect in 2010, and with bupropion as Auvelity for major depression in 2022.", "timeline": [["1950s", "Hoffmann-La Roche develops dextromethorphan as a non-addictive codeine substitute."], ["1958", "FDA approves dextromethorphan as a cough suppressant."], ["2010", "FDA approves dextromethorphan with quinidine (Nuedexta) for pseudobulbar affect."], ["2022", "FDA approves dextromethorphan with bupropion (Auvelity) for major depressive disorder."]], "cell": "Dextromethorphan acts in the brainstem cough center in the medulla to raise the threshold for the cough reflex. The exact receptor responsible for the antitussive effect is not fully settled; sigma-1 receptor agonism and NMDA receptor blockade are both thought to contribute, and it does not act meaningfully at mu opioid receptors. CYP2D6 in the liver O-demethylates it to dextrorphan, a more potent NMDA receptor channel blocker. At high doses, NMDA blockade produces a dissociative state similar to ketamine and phencyclidine. Dextromethorphan also blocks the serotonin transporter, which raises synaptic serotonin and explains its role in serotonin syndrome when combined with other serotonergic drugs.", "body": [["Brainstem cough center", "A higher threshold for the cough reflex means minor throat and airway irritation triggers fewer coughs."], ["Brain at high doses", "NMDA blockade by dextrorphan causes dizziness, confusion, a feeling of being outside the body and hallucinations, which is why it is misused in large amounts."], ["Serotonin system", "Serotonin reuptake blockade can add to SSRIs, MAO inhibitors and other serotonergic drugs and cause agitation, fever, tremor and clonus."], ["Gut", "Nausea and stomach upset are common minor effects."]], "journey": "Dextromethorphan is swallowed and absorbed quickly, with cough relief starting within about half an hour. It enters the brain easily. The liver changes it mainly by CYP2D6 into dextrorphan, with a smaller CYP3A4 pathway. Products leave in the urine. The half-life is a few hours in most people but much longer in CYP2D6 poor metabolizers. Quinidine, a strong CYP2D6 inhibitor, is added in Nuedexta on purpose to keep dextromethorphan levels up. Bupropion in Auvelity does the same thing.", "sidefx": "At usual doses side effects are mild: drowsiness, dizziness and nausea. Large doses cause the dissociative syndrome of NMDA blockade, with altered perception, hallucinations, ataxia, rapid eye movements (nystagmus) and, in severe cases, seizures. Serotonin transporter blockade explains serotonin syndrome when it is combined with MAO inhibitors or other serotonergic drugs. Combination cold products add hidden risks, such as acetaminophen liver injury or anticholinergic toxicity from antihistamines. Naloxone may partly reverse some toxicity in reports, but there is no reliable antidote; care is supportive.", "fun": "Dextromethorphan and the strong opioid levomethorphan are mirror images of each other. The left-handed version is a potent narcotic, while the right-handed version is a cough syrup ingredient.", "src": ["Delsym (dextromethorphan) OTC Drug Facts label", "Nuedexta FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]}});

;
